# Terms belonging to the tenants this repository is NOT. One extended regular expression per line;
# matched case-insensitively against every staged path and every staged byte. Whole-line comments
# and blank lines are ignored. See guards/tenant-guard.sh.
#
# This repository is the tenant-free layer, and it is public, which is what makes this file the one
# policy that cannot be written the obvious way: a list of the names a repository refuses is, read
# backwards, a list of what it is protecting. So the names are not here. This tracked half carries
# the *shapes* an identifier takes and the *words* that name a tenancy at all, both of which are
# safe to publish; the proper nouns live in an untracked `.protocol/tenant.local` beside it, which
# the guard appends without being able to tell the two apart (ARCHITECTURE.md §3).

# Identifier shapes that are always somebody's infrastructure
[0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{12}
(^|[^0-9.])(10|192\.168)\.[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3}
(^|[^0-9])[0-9]{12}([^0-9]|$)

# The words a tenancy is described with. Doctrine states rules as properties of the design, so a
# document that reaches for one of these has started arguing from whoever it was extracted from —
# which is the failure this repository exists to not have. A word used only to forbid itself reads
# the same to a guard as one used in earnest, so the prohibitions elsewhere say "a tenant, a machine
# or an account" and this line is why.
employer
employment
\bestate\b
corporate
consulting
day.?job
